1
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:04,700
[instrumental music]

2
00:00:04,787 --> 00:00:06,963
Welcome to the 23rd annual

3
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:09,574
Westing Resort
best chef challenge.

4
00:00:11,968 --> 00:00:14,101
[male #1]
'Don't burn those scallops.'

5
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:16,538
I'm Bertram Duesterberg,
your host.

6
00:00:16,625 --> 00:00:18,975
'Our competing chefs
today are'

7
00:00:19,062 --> 00:00:23,153
'first, from restaurant 182
in Sonoma..'

8
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:26,939
'The youngest winner ever of
the Schilling Palm d'cuisine'

9
00:00:27,027 --> 00:00:28,593
Chef Hannah Diaz.

10
00:00:33,381 --> 00:00:35,774
'From San Francisco,
where they host'

11
00:00:35,861 --> 00:00:39,082
the acclaimed public
radio show two top

12
00:00:39,169 --> 00:00:41,606
Chefs Arliss and
Julia St. Germain.

13
00:00:45,871 --> 00:00:47,830
[indistinct chatter]

14
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:52,139
Jeff, do you want
the mousse?

15
00:00:52,226 --> 00:00:54,663
- Jeff?
- Yeah, yeah.

16
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:56,621
Trained in Paris

17
00:00:56,708 --> 00:00:59,363
he is executive chef
at La Petite Maison

18
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:01,887
Chef Adrien Ludo!

19
00:01:06,066 --> 00:01:07,937
[indistinct chatter]

20
00:01:08,024 --> 00:01:11,201
Where's my chives?
Let's go, people!

21
00:01:11,288 --> 00:01:15,858
And first up
before our judges today..

22
00:01:15,945 --> 00:01:17,773
Okay, let's move!

23
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:20,036
- Come on, go, go!
- 'Hurry up, people.'

24
00:01:20,123 --> 00:01:21,603
Jeffrey Barge.

25
00:01:28,653 --> 00:01:30,002
Thank you.

26
00:01:37,749 --> 00:01:38,837
My..

27
00:01:38,924 --> 00:01:40,012
[clears throat]

28
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:43,886
My first, dish today, is..

29
00:01:47,455 --> 00:01:48,804
'...is, uh..'

30
00:01:52,199 --> 00:01:55,115
[all screaming]

31
00:01:55,202 --> 00:01:58,292
Oh, my goodness.
Call a doctor!

32
00:01:58,379 --> 00:02:00,598
Is there a doctor
in house, please?

33
00:02:00,685 --> 00:02:03,210
- Somebody call 9-1-1!
- Jeffrey!

34
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:06,909
[female #1]
'Jeffrey! Somebody!'

35
00:02:06,996 --> 00:02:09,781
'Jeffrey, say something!'

36
00:02:09,868 --> 00:02:11,827
'Can you hear me? Jeffrey?'

37
00:02:11,914 --> 00:02:15,483
Doctor, he's not moving.

38
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:17,006
[indistinct radio chatter]

39
00:02:21,228 --> 00:02:22,838
Anything from
the deputy coroner?

40
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:24,187
Just that he can't tell
us anything yet.

41
00:02:24,274 --> 00:02:25,884
Tell him we need to know.

42
00:02:25,971 --> 00:02:28,191
If this guy died of natural
causes we're going home.

43
00:02:28,278 --> 00:02:30,324
- Why so grumpy?
- I'm not.

44
00:02:30,411 --> 00:02:32,108
Why are we here? We don't
even know if it's a murder.

45
00:02:32,195 --> 00:02:34,763
One of the chefs called
her daddy who's a state senator

46
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:37,374
who called the AG,
who called the assistant AG

47
00:02:37,461 --> 00:02:39,507
who called me.
So we're here.

48
00:02:39,594 --> 00:02:40,856
I hate politics.

49
00:02:40,943 --> 00:02:42,466
This might go quick.

50
00:02:42,553 --> 00:02:43,859
Victim had a fistfight
with the event organizer.

51
00:02:43,946 --> 00:02:45,339
Happy days.

52
00:02:47,732 --> 00:02:49,299
[Bertram]
I want to be very clear.

53
00:02:49,386 --> 00:02:50,779
What happened was
sheerest accident.

54
00:02:50,866 --> 00:02:52,650
I did nothing wrong.

55
00:02:52,737 --> 00:02:55,697
Let's just stick to the facts,
Mr. Duesterberg.

56
00:02:55,784 --> 00:02:58,395
You said you had a fight
with the victim. About what?

57
00:02:58,482 --> 00:03:00,919
- Truffles.
- Truffles?

58
00:03:01,006 --> 00:03:02,921
Yes. They, they're
very pricey fungus.

59
00:03:03,008 --> 00:03:04,793
I know what a truffle is.

60
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:07,578
Uh, Jeffrey ordered a bundle
and he paid for them himself.

61
00:03:07,665 --> 00:03:09,319
A few hours
before the competition

62
00:03:09,406 --> 00:03:11,103
he couldn't find them.

63
00:03:11,191 --> 00:03:13,889
He demanded replacements,
which would have cost thousands.

64
00:03:13,976 --> 00:03:18,023
I said no. He started yelling
and then shoving.

65
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:19,808
Naturally, I defended myself.

66
00:03:19,895 --> 00:03:21,549
Very gently, of course.

67
00:03:21,636 --> 00:03:23,812
And then he, fell on his head?
Something like that?

68
00:03:23,899 --> 00:03:25,379
It's as I said it wa..
It was an accident.

69
00:03:25,466 --> 00:03:26,945
Oh, we'll see.

70
00:03:27,032 --> 00:03:29,731
Jeffrey sounds like a scrapper.
Quick with his fists?

71
00:03:29,818 --> 00:03:32,995
No, not really. He was..
He was a genius.

72
00:03:33,082 --> 00:03:35,171
Genius can be noisy.

73
00:03:35,258 --> 00:03:37,478
But he, he's never
laid hands on me before.

74
00:03:37,565 --> 00:03:40,220
Genius, really?

75
00:03:40,307 --> 00:03:41,308
Hmm.

76
00:03:41,395 --> 00:03:42,961
[clears throat]

77
00:03:43,048 --> 00:03:44,876
Hey, is he good for it?

78
00:03:44,963 --> 00:03:46,530
Not sure yet. What's that?

79
00:03:46,617 --> 00:03:48,228
I don't know. Some hors d'oeuvre
thing they're handing out.

80
00:03:48,315 --> 00:03:49,620
- Want one?
- No, thanks.

81
00:03:49,707 --> 00:03:51,013
I don't eat
anything I can't identify.

82
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:52,275
- Hmm.
- Excuse me.

83
00:03:52,362 --> 00:03:54,103
Is this the victim's dish?

84
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:56,627
Yes.

85
00:03:56,714 --> 00:03:57,889
Mmm.

86
00:03:59,151 --> 00:04:00,283
Mm-hmm.

87
00:04:02,503 --> 00:04:04,156
Excuse me.

88
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:05,723
[clears throat]

89
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:07,638
[grunting]

90
00:04:07,725 --> 00:04:09,292
Pardon.

91
00:04:09,379 --> 00:04:11,599
No genius there.
Waytoo much salt.

92
00:04:13,688 --> 00:04:14,863
Hmm.

93
00:04:16,168 --> 00:04:17,822
- Dr. Steiner.
- Hmm?

94
00:04:17,909 --> 00:04:20,172
Long time.
New threads. Sharp.

95
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:23,437
Yes. The move from Sacramento
has been quite pleasant.

96
00:04:23,524 --> 00:04:25,743
Fine wine, lovely weather,
no Patrick Jane

97
00:04:25,830 --> 00:04:27,179
or so I hoped.

98
00:04:27,267 --> 00:04:29,051
Cheeky.
Have you checked his mouth?

99
00:04:29,138 --> 00:04:31,662
For a possible head injury?
I don't think so.

100
00:04:31,749 --> 00:04:33,969
Don't be alarmed,
I amgoing to touch you.

101
00:04:34,056 --> 00:04:37,277
Mr. Jane, please,
I am in the..

102
00:04:37,364 --> 00:04:39,322
Yuck. Lisbon!

103
00:04:41,455 --> 00:04:43,021
Excuse me.

104
00:04:43,108 --> 00:04:45,110
What.. What made you think
to look there?

105
00:04:45,197 --> 00:04:47,678
Well, bad-tasting food
means bad taste buds.

106
00:04:49,027 --> 00:04:50,115
So much for the head wound.

107
00:04:50,202 --> 00:04:51,856
[Patrick]
'Hmm.'

108
00:04:51,943 --> 00:04:53,989
Duesterberg's off the hook.
He didn't die from a fall.

109
00:04:54,076 --> 00:04:55,599
Let's huddle together,
divvy up the interviews.

110
00:04:55,686 --> 00:04:57,514
What did kill him?

111
00:04:57,601 --> 00:04:59,037
Poison.

112
00:05:03,477 --> 00:05:04,608
[gags]

113
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,743
[theme music]

114
00:05:15,967 --> 00:05:17,621
Preliminary test came back.

115
00:05:17,708 --> 00:05:19,754
Deputy Coroner says that
the poison is probably ricin.

116
00:05:19,841 --> 00:05:21,277
Victim either
ate it or drank it.

117
00:05:21,364 --> 00:05:22,409
It makes sense.
If it was airborne

118
00:05:22,496 --> 00:05:24,019
people would be dropping
like flies.

119
00:05:24,106 --> 00:05:25,760
Ricin's easy enough to make.
Grind up some castor beans.

120
00:05:25,847 --> 00:05:27,675
Makes for a broad suspect pool.

121
00:05:27,762 --> 00:05:29,067
They tested the victim's food
there's no sign of it there

122
00:05:29,154 --> 00:05:30,155
or in the kitchen.

123
00:05:30,242 --> 00:05:31,679
The ricin takes hours to work.

124
00:05:31,766 --> 00:05:33,202
Must've been something
he ingested a while ago.

125
00:05:33,289 --> 00:05:34,899
No need to get
your stomach pumped then, huh?

126
00:05:34,986 --> 00:05:36,988
[phone ringing]
Ha, funny.

127
00:05:37,075 --> 00:05:39,034
You search the room. Test any
liquid or food that you find.

128
00:05:39,121 --> 00:05:40,644
Yeah, okay.

129
00:05:40,731 --> 00:05:43,343
Thanks. The victim's wife
just arrived.

130
00:05:43,430 --> 00:05:45,127
I'll handle it.
You talk to the other chefs.

131
00:05:45,214 --> 00:05:46,476
Duesterberg says that

132
00:05:46,563 --> 00:05:49,000
the winner of the contest
gets 100 grand.

133
00:05:53,831 --> 00:05:55,703
[Teresa]
'What are you doing?'

134
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:57,574
Oh, um..

135
00:05:57,661 --> 00:06:00,751
Trying to get the taste
of salty food out of my mouth.

136
00:06:00,838 --> 00:06:04,929
Pot de creme. It's basically
a pretentious chocolate pudding.

137
00:06:05,016 --> 00:06:06,714
- Very nice. You want some?
- No.

138
00:06:06,801 --> 00:06:09,281
We have to go and talk
to the victim's wife.

139
00:06:09,369 --> 00:06:12,197
Mmm. Well, maybe
she'll want some.

140
00:06:12,284 --> 00:06:13,416
Comfort food.

141
00:06:15,244 --> 00:06:16,898
[Arliss]
'Jeffrey was a dear friend.'

142
00:06:16,985 --> 00:06:18,421
We'd known each other for years.

143
00:06:18,508 --> 00:06:20,641
Which is why I had
my father bring you on.

144
00:06:20,728 --> 00:06:22,207
We want to find out
who did this.

145
00:06:22,294 --> 00:06:23,513
Interesting that
you called us in

146
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,210
before anyone knew
it was poison.

147
00:06:25,297 --> 00:06:26,690
You know something
everyone else didn't?

148
00:06:26,777 --> 00:06:29,867
No, I just knew
that something was wrong.

149
00:06:29,954 --> 00:06:32,304
Now you're all in competition
for a large cash prize.

150
00:06:32,392 --> 00:06:33,610
Helps that one of you is dead,
doesn't it?

151
00:06:33,697 --> 00:06:35,395
- 'Helps?'
- We eat together.

152
00:06:35,482 --> 00:06:36,918
We work together.
We're comrades.

153
00:06:37,005 --> 00:06:38,789
[Diaz chuckles]

154
00:06:38,876 --> 00:06:40,225
Something to add,
Ms. Diaz?

155
00:06:40,312 --> 00:06:42,576
Dude, if we're all
such comrades

156
00:06:42,663 --> 00:06:44,273
why'd you try and steal
Jeff's sous-chef?

157
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:46,275
Not true.

158
00:06:46,362 --> 00:06:47,755
Inquiries were made,
that's all.

159
00:06:47,842 --> 00:06:49,365
[chuckles]
Yeah, right.

160
00:06:49,452 --> 00:06:50,410
How'd you feel
about the victim?

161
00:06:50,497 --> 00:06:51,802
I hated his guts.

162
00:06:51,889 --> 00:06:54,457
Egotistical, manipulative

163
00:06:54,544 --> 00:06:56,372
maybe half as good
as he thought he was.

164
00:06:56,459 --> 00:06:58,722
Which would still make him
twice the chef that you are.

165
00:06:58,809 --> 00:06:59,941
Why don't you go
get another tattoo?

166
00:07:00,028 --> 00:07:01,551
Bite me, Julia.

167
00:07:01,638 --> 00:07:03,248
The delivery guy says
he left Jeffrey's truffles

168
00:07:03,335 --> 00:07:05,860
on his station this morning,
then they vanished.

169
00:07:05,947 --> 00:07:08,428
So who stole the truffles?

170
00:07:08,515 --> 00:07:10,430
[French accent]
Well, stealing truffles
makes one of us a murderer?

171
00:07:10,517 --> 00:07:11,605
It's a start.

172
00:07:11,692 --> 00:07:12,823
[scoffs]

173
00:07:15,217 --> 00:07:17,001
What?

174
00:07:17,088 --> 00:07:18,742
That is exactly the kind
of thing that hewould do.

175
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:20,788
He and Jeffrey
never really got along.

176
00:07:20,875 --> 00:07:22,442
You fat bastard.

177
00:07:22,529 --> 00:07:24,008
And truffles are
very expensive, aren't they?

178
00:07:24,095 --> 00:07:25,314
And you wouldn't
throw them out.

179
00:07:25,401 --> 00:07:27,011
Where does he keep his food?

180
00:07:27,098 --> 00:07:29,013
Down on the left side
of the pantry.

181
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:32,408
You know who would stop
at nothing to win? Those two.

182
00:07:32,495 --> 00:07:35,106
Ask them about the book deal
they've been chasing.

183
00:07:35,193 --> 00:07:37,500
'A competition prize
would help that, no?'

184
00:07:40,677 --> 00:07:41,635
[clears throat]

185
00:07:41,722 --> 00:07:43,680
Okay, fine. Arrest me.

186
00:07:43,767 --> 00:07:45,682
But I didn't
steal the truffles, okay?

187
00:07:45,769 --> 00:07:47,902
I rescued them
from a terrible, terrible fate.

188
00:07:47,989 --> 00:07:51,601
I see.

189
00:07:51,688 --> 00:07:54,430
Did Jeffrey have any problems
that you knew about

190
00:07:54,517 --> 00:07:55,692
personally or at work?

191
00:07:56,650 --> 00:07:57,781
[sighs]
Well, um..

192
00:07:59,783 --> 00:08:01,481
We were a problem.

193
00:08:03,439 --> 00:08:05,572
We're separated.

194
00:08:05,659 --> 00:08:07,269
I haven't spoken
to Jeffrey in months.

195
00:08:07,356 --> 00:08:08,705
What was the breakup about?

196
00:08:08,792 --> 00:08:11,273
Oh, god, what wasn't? Um..

197
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:13,405
Drinking, mostly.

198
00:08:13,493 --> 00:08:15,364
Occupational disease
for chefs.

199
00:08:15,451 --> 00:08:17,061
It's the pressure.
It wants a release.

200
00:08:17,148 --> 00:08:18,498
That, and, um..

201
00:08:18,585 --> 00:08:21,413
Don't listen to her,
whatever she says.

202
00:08:21,501 --> 00:08:22,850
She hates Jeff.

203
00:08:22,937 --> 00:08:25,679
- And you are?
- Victim's brother.

204
00:08:25,766 --> 00:08:28,159
Cheekbones.
Come on. You can see that.

205
00:08:28,246 --> 00:08:29,334
[mouthing]

206
00:08:29,421 --> 00:08:30,727
Uh, Kevin,
why don't we talk later?

207
00:08:30,814 --> 00:08:33,251
Yeah, I-I don't think so,
Abigail.

208
00:08:33,338 --> 00:08:35,689
My brother was
a freakin' genius, alright?

209
00:08:35,776 --> 00:08:38,300
You think anybody in my family
taught him how to cook?

210
00:08:38,387 --> 00:08:41,695
Please. He wanted to be a great
chef, and that's what he was.

211
00:08:41,782 --> 00:08:45,046
Did it all by himself.
And you?

212
00:08:45,133 --> 00:08:46,830
You were perfectly happy
to spend his money

213
00:08:46,917 --> 00:08:49,311
weren't you, Abigail?

214
00:08:49,398 --> 00:08:51,705
Your brother was a great man.
He elevated himself, didn't he?

215
00:08:51,792 --> 00:08:53,533
But you didn't.

216
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:57,101
That must've been cause for
some friction, I would think.

217
00:08:57,188 --> 00:08:59,451
'Did he leave you anything,
your brother?'

218
00:08:59,539 --> 00:09:02,106
Or did he leave it all
to the hated sister-in-law?

219
00:09:02,193 --> 00:09:03,412
- The money, the restaurant--
- Why don't you step back?

220
00:09:03,499 --> 00:09:04,935
Kevin, that's enough, alright?

221
00:09:05,022 --> 00:09:06,850
No, you don't tell me
what to do, okay?

222
00:09:06,937 --> 00:09:09,374
As you can see, drinking,
it runs in the family.

223
00:09:09,461 --> 00:09:12,247
[Kevin]
'Oh, s-see, that's how wrong
you are, actually.'

224
00:09:12,334 --> 00:09:15,816
Jeff was sober. He was
in the program for two weeks.

225
00:09:15,903 --> 00:09:17,731
Right. I've heard that before.

226
00:09:19,646 --> 00:09:21,299
It's probably why he called.

227
00:09:21,386 --> 00:09:22,823
You said
you hadn't talked to him.

228
00:09:22,910 --> 00:09:24,999
I-I didn't talk.

229
00:09:25,086 --> 00:09:27,567
Whoa. Two weeks sober.
That's impressive.

230
00:09:29,177 --> 00:09:31,179
And what else
are you hiding from us?

231
00:09:32,746 --> 00:09:34,835
Look, he called last night
at 12:30.

232
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:38,055
I hung up.

233
00:09:38,142 --> 00:09:39,666
What'd he say?

234
00:09:39,753 --> 00:09:41,929
He said..

235
00:09:42,016 --> 00:09:43,191
[sighs]

236
00:09:44,975 --> 00:09:48,239
[sighs]
He said he was sorry.

237
00:09:57,901 --> 00:09:58,902
[knocks on door]

238
00:09:58,989 --> 00:10:00,643
[Wayne]
'Hey.'

239
00:10:00,730 --> 00:10:02,863
Test for ricin.

240
00:10:02,950 --> 00:10:05,648
Yellow means positive,
nothing means..

241
00:10:05,735 --> 00:10:07,519
[sighs]

242
00:10:07,607 --> 00:10:08,738
...nothing.

243
00:10:10,914 --> 00:10:12,786
This place is quite a mess.

244
00:10:12,873 --> 00:10:14,701
What do you think
the intruder wanted?

245
00:10:14,788 --> 00:10:17,355
Nobody was looking
for anything.

246
00:10:17,442 --> 00:10:18,922
Jeffrey did this himself.

247
00:10:19,009 --> 00:10:20,794
And you know this, how?

248
00:10:20,881 --> 00:10:23,405
The punched-in mirror
is an act of self-hatred.

249
00:10:23,492 --> 00:10:25,668
Plus, all these drawers
are closed.

250
00:10:25,755 --> 00:10:27,670
A thoughtful ransacker
would close maybe one

251
00:10:27,757 --> 00:10:29,367
but all of them? No.

252
00:10:29,454 --> 00:10:32,109
Anyway.. Room's cleared.
No poison. We're moving on.

253
00:10:32,196 --> 00:10:33,328
[Patrick]
'That's nice.'

254
00:10:35,939 --> 00:10:38,986
Except you're not.
What's up?

255
00:10:39,073 --> 00:10:40,335
Well, Jeffrey
was recently sober

256
00:10:40,422 --> 00:10:41,815
but acting erratically.

257
00:10:41,902 --> 00:10:45,035
Demolishing his room,
picking fistfights

258
00:10:45,122 --> 00:10:46,950
calling his estranged wife
in the middle of the night

259
00:10:47,037 --> 00:10:47,995
you know what that's called?

260
00:10:48,082 --> 00:10:49,997
Relapse?

261
00:10:50,084 --> 00:10:53,087
Yes. And an addict on a relapse
will hide his stash.

262
00:10:57,352 --> 00:10:58,396
Ah.

263
00:10:58,483 --> 00:10:59,659
[chuckles]

264
00:11:03,184 --> 00:11:04,664
What better way
to poison a drunk

265
00:11:04,751 --> 00:11:06,274
than through his drink?

266
00:11:06,361 --> 00:11:07,579
Let's check it out.

267
00:11:07,667 --> 00:11:08,798
'Let's.'

268
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:16,458
There we go.

269
00:11:16,545 --> 00:11:17,764
Hold the applause.

270
00:11:21,768 --> 00:11:22,899
Bingo.

271
00:11:26,424 --> 00:11:27,556
[Patrick]
'Poison gin.'

272
00:11:29,950 --> 00:11:32,126
A smart way
to eliminate a rival.

273
00:11:35,085 --> 00:11:38,045
But not the act
of a gentleman, is it?

274
00:11:39,655 --> 00:11:41,178
[imitates French accent]
MonsieurLudo?

275
00:11:43,267 --> 00:11:44,965
Are you joking?

276
00:11:45,052 --> 00:11:48,620
Rigsby, take him in.

277
00:11:48,708 --> 00:11:50,884
[Ludo]
'This is not right.'

278
00:11:50,971 --> 00:11:52,929
I have done nothing.
I have done nothing.

279
00:11:53,016 --> 00:11:54,539
- Hang on.
- Come on, let's..

280
00:11:56,498 --> 00:11:57,673
[Patrick]
'She's the one
we need to talk to.'

281
00:11:57,760 --> 00:11:59,240
Me? Wh..

282
00:11:59,327 --> 00:12:01,329
You should've seen your reaction
when I accused Ludo..

283
00:12:01,416 --> 00:12:03,461
Pure relief.

284
00:12:03,548 --> 00:12:07,683
Oh, come on, please.
At least attempt denying it.

285
00:12:07,770 --> 00:12:10,381
- I didn't do it.
- She's the one.

286
00:12:10,468 --> 00:12:11,426
[Hannah sighs]

287
00:12:11,513 --> 00:12:13,733
Thank you.

288
00:12:13,820 --> 00:12:14,951
Merci.

289
00:12:19,564 --> 00:12:21,479
Why would I want
to kill Jeffrey?

290
00:12:21,566 --> 00:12:23,481
Winning a contest like this
would do a lot for a young woman

291
00:12:23,568 --> 00:12:24,874
trying to prove herself.

292
00:12:24,961 --> 00:12:26,615
I don't have anything
to prove.

293
00:12:26,702 --> 00:12:28,356
'You're a good cook.'

294
00:12:28,443 --> 00:12:30,401
Making ricin out of castor beans
wouldn't be hard, would it?

295
00:12:30,488 --> 00:12:32,099
No. But I didn't.

296
00:12:33,753 --> 00:12:35,842
[Kimball]
Take a look at this, Hanna.

297
00:12:35,929 --> 00:12:37,582
That's your credit card
statement.

298
00:12:38,932 --> 00:12:41,151
You bought gin.

299
00:12:41,238 --> 00:12:43,980
I'm over 21,
it's not a crime.

300
00:12:44,067 --> 00:12:45,721
[Kimball]
'You just happened to buy
yourself the same type of gin'

301
00:12:45,808 --> 00:12:46,766
'we found in the victim's room?'

302
00:12:46,853 --> 00:12:48,202
Look, can I go
or what? I..

303
00:12:48,289 --> 00:12:49,899
Hannah.

304
00:12:49,986 --> 00:12:52,162
We have enough to charge
you with murder right now.

305
00:12:52,249 --> 00:12:54,382
You need to talk to us,
tell us your side of the story.

306
00:12:56,688 --> 00:12:57,820
[sighs]

307
00:13:00,997 --> 00:13:02,520
I'm 28.

308
00:13:02,607 --> 00:13:04,261
I run my own kitchen.

309
00:13:05,741 --> 00:13:07,351
I don't have any..

310
00:13:08,352 --> 00:13:09,745
Food channel connections

311
00:13:09,832 --> 00:13:11,747
no cookbook
with my name on it.

312
00:13:13,183 --> 00:13:14,663
The Westing is my way up.

313
00:13:16,273 --> 00:13:17,492
[scoffs]

314
00:13:17,579 --> 00:13:19,320
Jeffrey was the one to beat.

315
00:13:20,582 --> 00:13:22,845
We all knew it.

316
00:13:22,932 --> 00:13:25,848
So I figured,
get him a drink.

317
00:13:27,502 --> 00:13:29,809
Why not?
Level the playing field.

318
00:13:31,593 --> 00:13:33,377
So, yeah,
I bought him the gin.

319
00:13:33,464 --> 00:13:35,379
But I have no idea
how the ricin got in there.

320
00:13:35,466 --> 00:13:37,164
Okay.

321
00:13:37,251 --> 00:13:39,993
I just tried
to get him drunk, that's all.

322
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:41,516
No poison.

323
00:13:44,911 --> 00:13:46,390
[sighs]

324
00:13:46,477 --> 00:13:48,610
Without anything else,
we can't hold her.

325
00:13:49,393 --> 00:13:50,699
What do you got?

326
00:13:50,786 --> 00:13:52,005
Jeffrey's phone records.

327
00:13:52,092 --> 00:13:54,007
Confirmed the call
with his wife.

328
00:13:55,312 --> 00:13:56,748
How long did they talk?

329
00:13:56,836 --> 00:13:59,926
'About five seconds.
Guess she did hang up.'

330
00:14:00,013 --> 00:14:01,666
But there's a second call
at 2:00 a.m.

331
00:14:01,753 --> 00:14:03,103
That one lasted
over an hour.

332
00:14:03,190 --> 00:14:05,757
- Who'd he call?
- His restaurant.

333
00:14:12,025 --> 00:14:13,809
Ms. Barge,
you have a minute?

334
00:14:15,115 --> 00:14:16,638
Yeah, sure. What's up?

335
00:14:16,725 --> 00:14:19,206
- Money troubles?
- How'd you guess?

336
00:14:19,293 --> 00:14:21,512
Well, it's a restaurant.
They always have money troubles.

337
00:14:21,599 --> 00:14:24,515
Well, this restaurant's
drowning in debt.

338
00:14:24,602 --> 00:14:27,605
Jeffrey was driving this place
off a cliff.

339
00:14:27,692 --> 00:14:29,042
It was all about the food.

340
00:14:30,652 --> 00:14:33,437
This is Eliza Greene,
Jeffrey's sous-chef.

341
00:14:33,524 --> 00:14:36,527
She's keeping things afloat
while I get my bearings..

342
00:14:36,614 --> 00:14:38,399
now that I own
a failing restaurant.

343
00:14:38,486 --> 00:14:40,575
[Eliza chuckles]

344
00:14:40,662 --> 00:14:42,229
Oh, sorry.

345
00:14:42,316 --> 00:14:45,667
Um, people would say that
the place is failing

346
00:14:45,754 --> 00:14:47,625
and, uh, Jeffrey would say

347
00:14:47,712 --> 00:14:49,976
yes, but failing
with style.

348
00:14:50,063 --> 00:14:52,630
Eliza, oh, you're the one
the others wanted to hire.

349
00:14:52,717 --> 00:14:54,458
You must be very good.

350
00:14:54,545 --> 00:14:55,982
I guess. I wouldn't know.

351
00:14:56,069 --> 00:14:57,853
False modesty?

352
00:14:57,940 --> 00:14:59,594
Jeffrey called the restaurant
last night at 2:00 a.m.

353
00:14:59,681 --> 00:15:01,204
Do you know
who he spoke to?

354
00:15:01,291 --> 00:15:04,251
Me. Oh, uh, he was considering
changing the menu

355
00:15:04,338 --> 00:15:06,601
for the contest,
wanted to talk about it.

356
00:15:06,688 --> 00:15:09,647
Couldn't that have waited
till the morning?

357
00:15:09,734 --> 00:15:12,172
That was just Jeff
being Jeff.

358
00:15:12,259 --> 00:15:13,825
He was always
after perfection.

359
00:15:13,913 --> 00:15:15,392
Irritating, getting
a phone call like that

360
00:15:15,479 --> 00:15:16,959
when you're on your way home
from work, isn't it?

361
00:15:17,046 --> 00:15:18,613
Not at all.

362
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:20,963
If you want
to make something great

363
00:15:21,050 --> 00:15:22,530
that's the job.

364
00:15:22,617 --> 00:15:23,748
[clears throat]
You know you should really

365
00:15:23,835 --> 00:15:25,011
get these, uh, deposits posted

366
00:15:25,098 --> 00:15:27,143
or the checks are gonna bounce.

367
00:15:27,230 --> 00:15:28,666
Thanks for your time.

368
00:15:29,841 --> 00:15:31,278
- 'Thank you.'
- Night.

369
00:15:31,365 --> 00:15:32,496
Bye.

370
00:15:32,583 --> 00:15:34,150
- Bye.
- Bye.

371
00:15:38,241 --> 00:15:39,851
Where are my keys?

372
00:15:41,375 --> 00:15:42,811
You picked my pocket?

373
00:15:42,898 --> 00:15:45,466
- My turn to drive.
- Jane!

374
00:15:45,553 --> 00:15:47,250
Oh, don't be
such an old lady. Get in.

375
00:15:48,599 --> 00:15:50,210
Your fears are groundless.

376
00:15:50,297 --> 00:15:52,690
You're right.
The car has air bags.

377
00:15:59,828 --> 00:16:01,264
[grunting]

378
00:16:01,351 --> 00:16:02,918
[screaming]

379
00:16:03,005 --> 00:16:04,137
Oh, no.

380
00:16:07,314 --> 00:16:09,925
Oh, bad move.

381
00:16:10,012 --> 00:16:11,666
[grunting]

382
00:16:11,753 --> 00:16:13,276
[groaning]

383
00:16:13,363 --> 00:16:14,625
[crickets chirping]

384
00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:17,715
[groaning]

385
00:16:25,027 --> 00:16:26,463
[Patrick]
'Oh.'

386
00:16:26,550 --> 00:16:27,682
[Teresa]
'Victim's brother?'

387
00:16:27,769 --> 00:16:30,250
[Patrick]
'Kevin.'

388
00:16:30,337 --> 00:16:34,080
Oh, I can't tell you how happy
I am that wasn't my car.

389
00:16:41,217 --> 00:16:42,610
You're out
of your freakin' mind.

390
00:16:42,697 --> 00:16:45,178
I would never, ever
harm my brother, ever.

391
00:16:45,265 --> 00:16:47,441
You assaulted
and robbed a girl, Kevin

392
00:16:47,528 --> 00:16:49,834
who ran your brother's
restaurant.

393
00:16:49,921 --> 00:16:52,620
You don't think that makes
you a little suspicious?

394
00:16:52,707 --> 00:16:54,709
That had nothing to do
with what happened to Jeff.

395
00:16:54,796 --> 00:16:56,667
I don't believe you.

396
00:16:56,754 --> 00:16:59,061
I can tell you why I went
after the money, alright

397
00:16:59,148 --> 00:17:00,758
but I'm gonna need
some guarantees.

398
00:17:00,845 --> 00:17:03,892
Oh, so we're negotiating now,
are we?

399
00:17:03,979 --> 00:17:06,199
I have valuable information.

400
00:17:06,286 --> 00:17:08,940
I can help you solve
Jeff's murder.

401
00:17:09,028 --> 00:17:11,117
But you're not
gonna tell us anything

402
00:17:11,204 --> 00:17:12,770
unless we cut you a deal?

403
00:17:16,557 --> 00:17:19,299
We're talking about
your brother here, Kevin.

404
00:17:19,386 --> 00:17:21,127
That's pretty low.

405
00:17:24,608 --> 00:17:26,349
You know what?
You're going to jail.

406
00:17:26,436 --> 00:17:27,785
You'll have plenty of time
to think about

407
00:17:27,872 --> 00:17:30,832
how you could've
done things differently.

408
00:17:33,791 --> 00:17:34,966
Wait.

409
00:17:38,361 --> 00:17:40,320
Sean Horlick.

410
00:17:40,407 --> 00:17:42,235
Jeff owed him money.

411
00:17:42,322 --> 00:17:43,584
He needed it
for the restaurant.

412
00:17:43,671 --> 00:17:45,194
Guy's a loan shark.

413
00:17:45,281 --> 00:17:48,545
I actually put them in touch
five months ago.

414
00:17:48,632 --> 00:17:50,460
I get a call yesterday
from Horlick

415
00:17:50,547 --> 00:17:52,810
and he says unless I want
to end up like Jeff

416
00:17:52,897 --> 00:17:54,551
I'd better get him
the money he owed.

417
00:17:54,638 --> 00:17:57,076
So you rob
your dead brother's wife?

418
00:17:57,163 --> 00:17:59,687
[scoffs]
She was always trying
to turn Jeff against me.

419
00:17:59,774 --> 00:18:01,515
Said I was a bad influence
or somethin'.

420
00:18:01,602 --> 00:18:05,127
Imagine that, huh?
Crazy.

421
00:18:05,214 --> 00:18:07,521
[sighs]
I knew their deposit schedule

422
00:18:07,608 --> 00:18:09,566
and they don't keep a gun.

423
00:18:09,653 --> 00:18:12,352
I figured
she screwed me before

424
00:18:12,439 --> 00:18:14,310
I screw her back.

425
00:18:14,397 --> 00:18:15,659
Sean Horlick.

426
00:18:15,746 --> 00:18:17,270
What else do you know
about him?

427
00:18:17,357 --> 00:18:19,446
Fraud, racketeering, extortion,

428
00:18:19,533 --> 00:18:21,274
couple of violent crimes
sprinkled in.

429
00:18:21,361 --> 00:18:23,841
Charges never seem to stick.
Horlick's smart.

430
00:18:23,928 --> 00:18:24,842
Track him down.
Take Rigsby.

431
00:18:24,929 --> 00:18:26,235
Okay.

432
00:18:26,322 --> 00:18:28,150
Cho, look into
Jeffrey's restaurant

433
00:18:28,237 --> 00:18:31,197
the financial records,
see who else he owed money to.

434
00:18:31,284 --> 00:18:33,068
[yawning]
I'll come with.

435
00:18:33,982 --> 00:18:36,985
I'm a mite peckish.

436
00:18:37,072 --> 00:18:38,943
What does thatmean?

437
00:18:39,030 --> 00:18:42,425
It means I'm hungry.

438
00:18:42,512 --> 00:18:44,471
- I can't read this.
- No?

439
00:18:47,517 --> 00:18:50,129
Oh, that says,
"March '09 accounts payable."

440
00:18:50,216 --> 00:18:52,131
- Oh!
- Hmm?

441
00:18:52,218 --> 00:18:55,046
This, this is just ridiculous.
This is delicious.

442
00:18:55,134 --> 00:18:57,832
[laughing]
Thank you.

443
00:18:57,919 --> 00:18:59,399
Let's hope you can keep
the quality up

444
00:18:59,486 --> 00:19:01,227
with the new boss.

445
00:19:01,314 --> 00:19:03,664
Abigail seems more concerned
about the money than the menu.

446
00:19:03,751 --> 00:19:06,145
Won't be a problem, I promise.

447
00:19:15,154 --> 00:19:16,851
For God's sake.
Did you try this?

448
00:19:16,938 --> 00:19:18,548
You don't like Abigail much,
do you?

449
00:19:18,635 --> 00:19:20,333
- 'Well.'
- Do it over.

450
00:19:22,378 --> 00:19:23,988
You're obviously
very talented.

451
00:19:24,075 --> 00:19:26,687
You should've struck out
on your own a long time ago

452
00:19:26,774 --> 00:19:28,863
unless there was
some reason to stay.

453
00:19:28,950 --> 00:19:32,258
I didtry to strike out
on my own, actually

454
00:19:32,345 --> 00:19:33,955
couple months ago.

455
00:19:34,042 --> 00:19:35,478
- Failed with style?
- 'I wish.'

456
00:19:35,565 --> 00:19:37,654
State wouldn't
grant me my liquor license.

457
00:19:37,741 --> 00:19:41,354
[chuckles]
No license,
no investors, so..

458
00:19:41,441 --> 00:19:43,356
'Luckily, Jeffrey
took me back again.'

459
00:19:43,443 --> 00:19:44,618
So why not hook up
with him?

460
00:19:44,705 --> 00:19:46,533
He was separated,
available.

461
00:19:46,620 --> 00:19:50,319
[chuckles]
I've never liked
waiting in lines.

462
00:19:52,626 --> 00:19:54,584
Excuse me.
I've got prep work to do.

463
00:19:54,671 --> 00:19:56,717
- Thanks for lunch.
- Uh-huh.

464
00:19:56,804 --> 00:19:59,110
'I didn't see any other loans on
the books. It's a good thing.'

465
00:19:59,198 --> 00:20:01,200
Jeffrey wasn't even taking
a salary the last few months.

466
00:20:01,287 --> 00:20:03,114
He wasn't? Hmm.

467
00:20:04,812 --> 00:20:08,555
We're gonna have to
make a stop on the way back.

468
00:20:08,642 --> 00:20:09,991
[Wayne sighs]

469
00:20:12,994 --> 00:20:14,909
Huh. Magic brownies.

470
00:20:17,172 --> 00:20:19,740
This is more...clean-cut
than I'd expected.

471
00:20:19,827 --> 00:20:21,959
Yeah, medical pot,
it's a growth industry.

472
00:20:22,046 --> 00:20:23,657
[Josh]
'First timers, huh?'

473
00:20:23,744 --> 00:20:25,789
I'll tell you the same thing
I tell all the newbies.

474
00:20:25,876 --> 00:20:27,269
go for today's specials.

475
00:20:27,356 --> 00:20:28,531
Sure, they're
a couple bucks more

476
00:20:28,618 --> 00:20:30,272
but between you and me
and the wall

477
00:20:30,359 --> 00:20:32,970
it's the freshest herb we got.
Plus it's locally grown.

478
00:20:33,057 --> 00:20:34,494
Who doesn't wanna support
local farmers?

479
00:20:34,581 --> 00:20:36,583
CBI. Where's your boss?

480
00:20:36,670 --> 00:20:38,541
Uh, Mr. Horlick?
I couldn't say.

481
00:20:40,543 --> 00:20:43,198
But, uh, our permits are all
up to date by the book, 100%.

482
00:20:43,285 --> 00:20:46,375
Well, Mr. Horlick's a suspect
in a murder investigation, Josh

483
00:20:46,462 --> 00:20:48,899
so you if you know where he is,
you should tell us right now.

484
00:20:48,986 --> 00:20:52,033
Is, uh, there a problem here?

485
00:20:52,120 --> 00:20:53,730
We're here about
Jeffrey Barge.

486
00:20:53,817 --> 00:20:56,994
Who? Oh. Oh, yeah.

487
00:20:57,081 --> 00:20:58,953
Yeah, Jeff. Sure.
Sure. What about him?

488
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:00,737
Jeffrey died yesterday.

489
00:21:00,824 --> 00:21:05,002
Yeah. Yeah, I know. I saw that
on the news. Wow. Heavy.

490
00:21:05,089 --> 00:21:09,050
I mean, boom, he's here.
He's gone.

491
00:21:09,137 --> 00:21:11,792
And how about that?
Life, huh?

492
00:21:11,879 --> 00:21:13,620
We know you lent him money,
Mr. Horlick.

493
00:21:13,707 --> 00:21:15,970
Well, how do you know that?
I mean, who says?

494
00:21:16,057 --> 00:21:17,580
I'll take that as a yes.

495
00:21:17,667 --> 00:21:20,888
Frosty one, aren't we?
I like it.

496
00:21:20,975 --> 00:21:23,369
You like jail, too?

497
00:21:23,456 --> 00:21:25,196
Yeah, that's right.
Kevin told us everything.

498
00:21:25,284 --> 00:21:28,025
It's about time
you did as well.

499
00:21:28,112 --> 00:21:30,158
Fine.

500
00:21:30,245 --> 00:21:32,029
I called him
about the money.

501
00:21:32,116 --> 00:21:35,032
Obviously, he drew some sinister
and incorrect implications

502
00:21:35,119 --> 00:21:36,338
from our conversation

503
00:21:36,425 --> 00:21:39,298
but it was all
a big misunderstanding.

504
00:21:39,385 --> 00:21:42,083
Chalk it up to my,
my poor communication skills.

505
00:21:42,170 --> 00:21:43,389
I think you communicate
very well.

506
00:21:43,476 --> 00:21:44,999
Yes, you do.
You have sleazebag

507
00:21:45,086 --> 00:21:46,740
written across your forehead
in big letters.

508
00:21:46,827 --> 00:21:48,481
Why would I kill Jeff?

509
00:21:48,568 --> 00:21:50,744
You don't off someone
who can pay you back.

510
00:21:50,831 --> 00:21:52,615
I mean, the guy practically
lived at the Ritz.

511
00:21:52,702 --> 00:21:54,138
What are you talking about?
Jeff was broke.

512
00:21:54,225 --> 00:21:56,924
Yeah, that's what he wanted me
to think, too.

513
00:21:57,011 --> 00:21:59,622
But I got a buddy who works
security at the hotel.

514
00:21:59,709 --> 00:22:02,408
He saw the deadbeat
staying there a bunch of times.

515
00:22:02,495 --> 00:22:04,540
You don't stay there unless
your pockets are padded.

516
00:22:06,629 --> 00:22:10,459
I'd like to take yousometime,
thaw you out a little bit.

517
00:22:10,546 --> 00:22:12,331
Yeah, that'll happen.

518
00:22:15,856 --> 00:22:18,119
We got something.

519
00:22:18,206 --> 00:22:19,947
Jeffrey stayed at the Ritz
over a dozen times

520
00:22:20,034 --> 00:22:22,558
in the past two months,
paid in cash every time.

521
00:22:22,645 --> 00:22:24,168
Looks like an affair.

522
00:22:24,255 --> 00:22:26,214
But Jeffrey and his wife
lived separate lives.

523
00:22:26,301 --> 00:22:27,998
Why would he care
if there was a record of it?

524
00:22:28,085 --> 00:22:29,609
Maybe his partner cared.

525
00:22:29,696 --> 00:22:31,088
Because she's married.

526
00:22:31,175 --> 00:22:32,829
She had the money.
She paid for the room

527
00:22:32,916 --> 00:22:35,179
but she paid in cash so
her husband wouldn't find out.

528
00:22:35,266 --> 00:22:37,617
Keep digging.
Find out who the woman was.

529
00:22:41,011 --> 00:22:43,362
[indistinct chatter]

530
00:22:45,973 --> 00:22:48,192
Mmm. Next round's
tomorrow, huh?

531
00:22:49,890 --> 00:22:51,892
Keep workin'.
You'll get there.

532
00:22:52,762 --> 00:22:54,373
Merci beaucoup.

533
00:22:55,199 --> 00:22:57,201
Arliss.

534
00:22:57,288 --> 00:22:59,247
[Patrick]
'Careful, careful.'

535
00:23:01,858 --> 00:23:04,252
Oh, that smells good.
What is that?

536
00:23:04,339 --> 00:23:06,080
Knock yourself out.

537
00:23:06,167 --> 00:23:08,343
It's not for the competition.
It's my lunch.

538
00:23:08,430 --> 00:23:09,736
I'd advise against that.

539
00:23:09,823 --> 00:23:11,651
Don't listen to him.
He's a coward.

540
00:23:11,738 --> 00:23:14,175
- Is he?
- It's the Red Savina pepper.

541
00:23:14,262 --> 00:23:16,351
- It's kinda spicy.
- 'Kind of?'

542
00:23:16,438 --> 00:23:18,048
It's the hottest pepper
known to man. Trust me.

543
00:23:18,135 --> 00:23:19,615
Leave it alone.

544
00:23:19,702 --> 00:23:22,488
It's good on everything
meat, fruit, soups.

545
00:23:22,575 --> 00:23:25,491
Not that any of the people
would know. Babies.

546
00:23:25,578 --> 00:23:27,493
Well, I'd love a little try
of that. Thank you.

547
00:23:27,580 --> 00:23:30,713
Mr. Jane,
our chefs are extremely busy.

548
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:32,498
Would you mind coming back
another time?

549
00:23:32,585 --> 00:23:35,196
You're quite the ghoul,
aren't you, Duesterberg?

550
00:23:35,283 --> 00:23:37,154
Dragging on
this meaningless contest

551
00:23:37,241 --> 00:23:38,808
after a man
has been killed.

552
00:23:38,895 --> 00:23:42,551
This contest, Mr. Jane,
is an affirmation of life.

553
00:23:42,638 --> 00:23:45,728
It's a testament that
appetite still exists

554
00:23:45,815 --> 00:23:46,947
even in the face of death.

555
00:23:47,034 --> 00:23:48,514
And you need the money.

556
00:23:48,601 --> 00:23:50,298
- Time for you to go.
- Fine.

557
00:23:50,385 --> 00:23:53,257
I have all the information
I need, thank you very much.

558
00:23:53,344 --> 00:23:55,564
He lies. He hasn't asked
one single question.

559
00:23:55,651 --> 00:23:57,871
Why would I? I can learn
everything I need to learn

560
00:23:57,958 --> 00:24:00,308
from watching you all cook.

561
00:24:00,395 --> 00:24:02,876
You, for example,

562
00:24:02,963 --> 00:24:05,966
use lemon to the point
of excess.

563
00:24:06,053 --> 00:24:09,535
'Tart, aggressive flavors,
sublimated aggression'

564
00:24:09,622 --> 00:24:12,276
'toward an important figure
in your life.'

565
00:24:12,363 --> 00:24:13,887
Obviously,
you're a daddy's girl

566
00:24:13,974 --> 00:24:14,975
so I'm gonna go
with mother.

567
00:24:15,062 --> 00:24:16,498
No, let me correct that..

568
00:24:18,369 --> 00:24:19,806
'...stepmother.'

569
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:22,199
'Ludo!'

570
00:24:22,286 --> 00:24:24,114
You were adopted, no?

571
00:24:24,201 --> 00:24:26,160
No. I wasn't.

572
00:24:26,247 --> 00:24:29,250
Took a shot. Moving on.

573
00:24:29,337 --> 00:24:31,295
You were having an affair
with Jeffrey.

574
00:24:31,382 --> 00:24:33,689
'Weren't you?'

575
00:24:33,776 --> 00:24:35,082
What?

576
00:24:35,169 --> 00:24:36,910
You can tell all this
by the food?

577
00:24:36,997 --> 00:24:38,564
[Patrick]
'Well, they cooked together
every day'

578
00:24:38,651 --> 00:24:40,914
yet their kitchen
rhythms are off.

579
00:24:41,001 --> 00:24:44,744
They're bashing into each other,
getting in each other's way.

580
00:24:44,831 --> 00:24:47,050
You're not paying enough
attention to her, Arliss.

581
00:24:47,137 --> 00:24:48,269
No wonder she strayed.

582
00:24:48,356 --> 00:24:49,923
That's nonsense.

583
00:24:50,010 --> 00:24:52,969
Really? Then why did
your wife have her father put

584
00:24:53,056 --> 00:24:55,581
the state's best police force
on this case?

585
00:24:55,668 --> 00:24:57,017
Look at her, Arliss.

586
00:24:57,104 --> 00:24:58,453
'She's made no attempt'

587
00:24:58,540 --> 00:25:00,499
no attempt at all
to deny it.

588
00:25:02,109 --> 00:25:03,284
Is it true?

589
00:25:03,371 --> 00:25:05,025
- I..
- So I take it you know

590
00:25:05,112 --> 00:25:06,722
about Julia's affair
with the victim now.

591
00:25:06,809 --> 00:25:08,289
Oh, roger that.

592
00:25:14,121 --> 00:25:16,863
Jeff and I got together
about five months ago

593
00:25:16,950 --> 00:25:19,169
after he separated
from his wife.

594
00:25:20,780 --> 00:25:22,825
[Patrick]
'But he ended it.'

595
00:25:22,912 --> 00:25:24,131
Yes.

596
00:25:24,218 --> 00:25:26,046
When?

597
00:25:26,133 --> 00:25:28,004
The night before he died.

598
00:25:28,091 --> 00:25:30,180
'I went to his room
at the resort.'

599
00:25:30,267 --> 00:25:33,357
'He was drunk. I thought
he was trying to get sober'

600
00:25:33,444 --> 00:25:36,056
but I guess
not that night.

601
00:25:36,143 --> 00:25:38,449
Jeff didn't
handle stress well.

602
00:25:38,537 --> 00:25:40,669
Must have made you angry.

603
00:25:40,756 --> 00:25:43,280
No. I accepted it.

604
00:25:44,499 --> 00:25:46,109
I loved him.

605
00:25:46,196 --> 00:25:49,852
But he was a drunk.
Better left alone.

606
00:25:49,939 --> 00:25:51,637
Wise of you.

607
00:25:51,724 --> 00:25:54,422
I asked for a glass
of water.

608
00:25:54,509 --> 00:25:58,774
I'm sure it'll be here
in a minute.

609
00:25:58,861 --> 00:26:01,951
You think I'm entitled
and snippy and rich.

610
00:26:02,038 --> 00:26:03,562
My father's a senator.

611
00:26:03,649 --> 00:26:05,912
I don't have to take crap
from people like you.

612
00:26:05,999 --> 00:26:07,304
I'm just trying to figure out

613
00:26:07,391 --> 00:26:09,263
who killed Jeffrey Barge,
that's all.

614
00:26:09,350 --> 00:26:10,656
I didn't kill him.

615
00:26:10,743 --> 00:26:13,136
I don't believe you, Julia,
not yet.

616
00:26:13,223 --> 00:26:14,616
[sighs]

617
00:26:14,703 --> 00:26:16,923
I really need
that glass of water.

618
00:26:17,750 --> 00:26:19,142
I'll get it.

619
00:26:19,229 --> 00:26:22,493
We still have a few more
questions for you.

620
00:26:22,581 --> 00:26:24,017
[Patrick]
'Lisbon!'

621
00:26:24,104 --> 00:26:26,019
9-1-1.

622
00:26:26,106 --> 00:26:28,108
Julia? Julia?

623
00:26:28,195 --> 00:26:31,154
Julia. Can you hear me?

624
00:26:31,241 --> 00:26:33,374
Are you on medication?

625
00:26:33,461 --> 00:26:35,071
Come on. Answer.

626
00:26:36,290 --> 00:26:37,770
How many fingers
am I holding up?

627
00:26:38,466 --> 00:26:39,598
'Julia!'

628
00:26:44,733 --> 00:26:46,692
[Teresa]
Yes. Uh-huh.

629
00:26:46,779 --> 00:26:49,390
I know. Yes.

630
00:26:49,477 --> 00:26:55,178
Mr. Attorney General, sir,
I.. I-I understand completely.

631
00:26:55,265 --> 00:26:57,267
I will.

632
00:26:57,354 --> 00:27:00,140
She's straightening.
Straightening her desk.

633
00:27:00,227 --> 00:27:01,445
Uh-oh.

634
00:27:01,532 --> 00:27:03,012
Absolutely.

635
00:27:03,099 --> 00:27:05,362
Coroner's office
says it was ricin poisoning

636
00:27:05,449 --> 00:27:06,886
uh, just like Jeffrey.

637
00:27:06,973 --> 00:27:08,670
Guess they were both
poisoned at the hotel.

638
00:27:10,063 --> 00:27:12,239
Oh, crap.
Is she straightening?

639
00:27:12,326 --> 00:27:14,850
Victim was a senator's daughter.
AG must be livid.

640
00:27:14,937 --> 00:27:17,636
Ugh. It's not our fault.
Ricin takes hours to kick in.

641
00:27:17,723 --> 00:27:19,028
[Wayne]
'Yeah, tell it to the AG.'

642
00:27:19,115 --> 00:27:20,639
[sighs]
What's going on
with the search?

643
00:27:20,726 --> 00:27:22,423
Local sheriff's working on it

644
00:27:22,510 --> 00:27:24,077
taking every food and liquid
off the resort to be tested.

645
00:27:24,164 --> 00:27:27,602
Resort, the contest,
it's all been shut down.

646
00:27:27,689 --> 00:27:31,127
News flash. The AG wants
this case closed now.

647
00:27:31,214 --> 00:27:32,346
Well, we've got
two dead married lovers.

648
00:27:32,433 --> 00:27:33,739
I'd say the killer's a spouse.

649
00:27:33,826 --> 00:27:35,262
Let's start with that.

650
00:27:38,047 --> 00:27:39,440
[Kimball]
There he is.

651
00:27:41,181 --> 00:27:43,531
Cinnamon,
sage and coriander

652
00:27:43,618 --> 00:27:45,881
a squeeze of lemon,
then butter.

653
00:27:47,274 --> 00:27:49,276
No, no garlic.

654
00:27:49,363 --> 00:27:51,321
Not at all. Ciao.

655
00:27:51,408 --> 00:27:53,193
Idiot.

656
00:27:53,280 --> 00:27:55,543
Your wife just died, and you're
talking about recipes?

657
00:27:55,630 --> 00:27:57,197
What would you have me do?

658
00:27:57,284 --> 00:27:59,286
Sir, we'd like to discuss
your wife's estate.

659
00:27:59,373 --> 00:28:01,592
Yes. Sorry.

660
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:04,421
Idiots. Garlic.

661
00:28:04,508 --> 00:28:06,380
So Julia had money.
So what?

662
00:28:06,467 --> 00:28:08,077
Well, you stand to inherit
all of it, isn't that right?

663
00:28:08,164 --> 00:28:09,644
Yes. So?

664
00:28:09,731 --> 00:28:11,211
Your wife and her lover
are now both dead

665
00:28:11,298 --> 00:28:13,604
killed the same way. That makes
you our number one guy.

666
00:28:13,692 --> 00:28:15,041
Or number two, I'd say.

667
00:28:15,128 --> 00:28:16,216
Have you talked to Abigail yet?

668
00:28:16,303 --> 00:28:17,696
[Wayne]
'Why?'

669
00:28:17,783 --> 00:28:19,610
Ask her about the fight
she and Jeffrey had

670
00:28:19,698 --> 00:28:21,438
at the Fairplex Market
two weeks ago.

671
00:28:21,525 --> 00:28:22,701
It was quite a scene.

672
00:28:22,788 --> 00:28:24,441
You didn't think
to mention this earlier?

673
00:28:24,528 --> 00:28:27,270
Interrogation focuses the mind.
What can I say?

674
00:28:27,357 --> 00:28:28,837
Except you're lying.

675
00:28:28,924 --> 00:28:31,187
Jeffrey hadn't seen his wife
in months.

676
00:28:31,274 --> 00:28:33,886
If Abigail said that,
she'sthe liar.

677
00:28:35,931 --> 00:28:38,281
The market clerk
confirmed the story.

678
00:28:38,368 --> 00:28:40,414
You were in contact with Jeff,
and you were fighting.

679
00:28:40,501 --> 00:28:41,981
Look, I did not kill Jeffrey

680
00:28:42,068 --> 00:28:44,026
if that's what
you're getting at.

681
00:28:44,113 --> 00:28:46,768
'I didn't even know
about this thing with Julia.'

682
00:28:46,855 --> 00:28:48,465
What were you fighting about?

683
00:28:48,552 --> 00:28:50,685
You've really changed
your look, Abigail.

684
00:28:50,772 --> 00:28:52,034
'Haven't you?'

685
00:28:52,121 --> 00:28:53,732
Not that you don't
look lovely now.

686
00:28:53,819 --> 00:28:58,214
It's just this old look, uh,
accentuated your assets.

687
00:28:58,301 --> 00:29:00,739
My husband just died, okay?

688
00:29:00,826 --> 00:29:03,306
You're still putting in time
with the make-up though, I see.

689
00:29:03,393 --> 00:29:05,439
So appearance is important
to you.

690
00:29:05,526 --> 00:29:06,832
What are you getting at?

691
00:29:06,919 --> 00:29:08,311
She's put on 5 or 6 pounds

692
00:29:08,398 --> 00:29:10,009
doesn't fit into
the sleek outfits anymore

693
00:29:10,096 --> 00:29:11,401
so she wears this.

694
00:29:11,488 --> 00:29:13,316
She's pregnant. Right?

695
00:29:13,403 --> 00:29:15,449
'Congratulations.
First trimester.'

696
00:29:16,580 --> 00:29:18,234
Jeff's child, right?

697
00:29:18,974 --> 00:29:20,106
[sighs]

698
00:29:21,150 --> 00:29:22,456
Right?

699
00:29:25,502 --> 00:29:29,245
A few months ago, he came by
to pick up some stuff.

700
00:29:32,205 --> 00:29:34,120
Things happened.

701
00:29:38,037 --> 00:29:40,300
And two weeks ago,
I took a pregnancy test.

702
00:29:40,387 --> 00:29:43,346
How did he take the news
of impending fatherhood?

703
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:46,741
Okay.

704
00:29:46,828 --> 00:29:48,961
That's what you were
fighting about.

705
00:29:50,397 --> 00:29:52,138
I don't know why
I didn't tell you.

706
00:29:54,183 --> 00:29:55,794
I've been, uh..

707
00:29:57,621 --> 00:29:58,797
...confused.

708
00:30:01,364 --> 00:30:03,323
My parents don't even know.

709
00:30:04,454 --> 00:30:06,152
[sighs]

710
00:30:06,239 --> 00:30:09,068
Do us a favor, Abigail.

711
00:30:09,155 --> 00:30:10,721
We're gonna need to search
your house.

712
00:30:10,809 --> 00:30:13,899
Save us all some time
and grant us permission.

713
00:30:13,986 --> 00:30:15,117
[birds chirping]

714
00:30:20,209 --> 00:30:21,689
Did you know
a full-grown elephant

715
00:30:21,776 --> 00:30:25,432
can eat up to 495 pounds
of food per day?

716
00:30:25,519 --> 00:30:29,044
Could you at least
pretend to be helping?

717
00:30:29,131 --> 00:30:30,698
Got somethin'.

718
00:30:30,785 --> 00:30:32,395
Found this in the garage.

719
00:30:32,482 --> 00:30:33,832
Nice work.

720
00:30:33,919 --> 00:30:36,008
And we care about
a bag of beans why?

721
00:30:36,095 --> 00:30:37,879
Castor beans.
They're used to make ricin.

722
00:30:37,966 --> 00:30:39,663
Ah, of course.

723
00:30:41,752 --> 00:30:43,319
Naughty Abigail.

724
00:30:45,147 --> 00:30:48,934
We found traces of ricin
in Julia's favorite red pepper.

725
00:30:49,021 --> 00:30:51,632
Her death was not accidental.

726
00:30:51,719 --> 00:30:55,679
Both her and your husband
were specifically targeted.

727
00:30:55,766 --> 00:30:57,507
Together with the castor
beans we found

728
00:30:57,594 --> 00:30:59,031
it's not looking good for you.

729
00:30:59,118 --> 00:31:02,425
My client denies any involvement
in these killings.

730
00:31:02,512 --> 00:31:04,427
She bore no malice
towards her husband

731
00:31:04,514 --> 00:31:05,951
or Ms. St. Germain.

732
00:31:06,038 --> 00:31:07,648
No malice
toward her alcoholic

733
00:31:07,735 --> 00:31:10,520
and fiscally irresponsible
husband and his lover?

734
00:31:10,607 --> 00:31:13,262
'She was not aware
of that relationship'

735
00:31:13,349 --> 00:31:15,874
nor did she ever set foot
in that resort.

736
00:31:15,961 --> 00:31:18,137
- Which you can't prove.
- I didn't do this..

737
00:31:18,224 --> 00:31:19,965
Then how do you explain
the castor beans in your house?

738
00:31:20,052 --> 00:31:21,488
She had no knowledge of them.

739
00:31:21,575 --> 00:31:23,359
That's convenient.

740
00:31:23,446 --> 00:31:27,363
This is a grieving widow,
a pregnant, grieving widow.

741
00:31:27,450 --> 00:31:29,626
'You think that a jury
is going to convict'

742
00:31:29,713 --> 00:31:32,586
on this circumstantial Traif?

743
00:31:32,673 --> 00:31:35,154
We can make this stick,
and you know it.

744
00:31:35,241 --> 00:31:37,243
Believe me, you will feel
much better if you..

745
00:31:37,330 --> 00:31:38,505
[phone ringing]

746
00:31:38,592 --> 00:31:40,028
Would you excuse me?

747
00:31:42,639 --> 00:31:47,296
What?

748
00:31:47,383 --> 00:31:49,429
- What emergency?
- Follow me.

749
00:31:49,516 --> 00:31:50,909
Jane, I don't really
have time for this.

750
00:31:50,996 --> 00:31:52,693
I was working on getting
a confession.

751
00:31:52,780 --> 00:31:54,477
- This is far more important.
- What is?

752
00:31:54,564 --> 00:31:56,915
Agents, welcome.
It's such a relief to know

753
00:31:57,002 --> 00:31:58,133
that the killer
has been caught.

754
00:31:58,220 --> 00:32:00,179
Just doing our job.

755
00:32:00,266 --> 00:32:02,355
And thank you for coming to
this little memorial dinner.

756
00:32:02,442 --> 00:32:03,530
Dinner?

757
00:32:03,617 --> 00:32:06,402
Honoring Jeffrey and Julia.

758
00:32:06,489 --> 00:32:07,926
We're having dinner?

759
00:32:08,013 --> 00:32:09,579
Gotta eat.

760
00:32:11,842 --> 00:32:13,888
I certainly hope
you enjoy yourself.

761
00:32:13,975 --> 00:32:16,935
It should be delicious.
Excuse me.

762
00:32:17,022 --> 00:32:19,285
Those serviettes
are horrifying.

763
00:32:19,372 --> 00:32:21,417
You have five seconds
to tell me what's going on.

764
00:32:21,504 --> 00:32:23,419
First, we need a drink.

765
00:32:23,506 --> 00:32:25,160
Is it that bad?

766
00:32:27,989 --> 00:32:30,513
It's lovely.

767
00:32:30,600 --> 00:32:31,732
- '...finely chopped.'
- 'We're gonna need more soup.'

768
00:32:33,168 --> 00:32:34,778
Not bad, Ludo.

769
00:32:34,865 --> 00:32:36,302
Can we get
the bowls ready?

770
00:32:36,389 --> 00:32:37,999
You shouldn't be here.

771
00:32:38,086 --> 00:32:40,523
Yeah, you're probably right.
You gonna make me leave?

772
00:32:40,610 --> 00:32:41,872
I hope not.

773
00:32:41,960 --> 00:32:43,439
Excuse me.

774
00:32:43,526 --> 00:32:44,875
Oh, excuse me.

775
00:32:44,963 --> 00:32:46,225
I thought you'd be
a little more grateful.

776
00:32:46,312 --> 00:32:48,009
I did just catch
your friend's killer.

777
00:32:48,096 --> 00:32:50,533
We're grateful.
Just a little busy.

778
00:32:50,620 --> 00:32:53,145
Is that it?

779
00:32:53,232 --> 00:32:54,973
- Oh, that smells great.
- Not now, s'il vous plait.

780
00:32:55,060 --> 00:32:56,017
Okay, okay.

781
00:32:57,497 --> 00:32:59,673
Oh, oops! Whoa! Oops!

782
00:32:59,760 --> 00:33:02,415
Fire! Fire! Fire!

783
00:33:03,416 --> 00:33:06,158
Quick! Aah!

784
00:33:06,245 --> 00:33:08,073
Whoa! Ah!

785
00:33:08,160 --> 00:33:11,337
You know, I-I'm just gonna
leave you guys with that.

786
00:33:11,424 --> 00:33:12,816
Aah!

787
00:33:16,820 --> 00:33:18,692
Alright, everyone, please.

788
00:33:18,779 --> 00:33:21,477
Lift your glass.

789
00:33:21,564 --> 00:33:26,265
To Jeffrey and Julia,
friends, colleagues

790
00:33:26,352 --> 00:33:29,703
titans in the culinary world

791
00:33:29,790 --> 00:33:32,706
'you will not be
soon forgotten.'

792
00:33:32,793 --> 00:33:34,012
- Salud.
- Salud.

793
00:33:34,099 --> 00:33:36,014
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

794
00:33:36,101 --> 00:33:38,320
Ahh.

795
00:33:38,407 --> 00:33:40,061
Mm-hmm.

796
00:33:40,148 --> 00:33:41,454
To begin,
we have a melon soup

797
00:33:41,541 --> 00:33:45,371
'Prosciutto di Parma,
mint and basil.'

798
00:33:45,458 --> 00:33:47,199
Mmm.

799
00:33:47,286 --> 00:33:50,289
A reinterpretation
of one of Julia's specialties.

800
00:33:50,376 --> 00:33:52,073
Bon appetite.

801
00:33:52,160 --> 00:33:53,683
Thank you.

802
00:33:55,381 --> 00:33:57,818
Come on. Don't let
your Midwestern taste buds

803
00:33:57,905 --> 00:33:59,167
'hold you back.'

804
00:34:01,996 --> 00:34:03,258
[coughing]

805
00:34:03,345 --> 00:34:05,565
Oh, chef.

806
00:34:05,652 --> 00:34:08,263
- What is this spice?
- 'Spice?'

807
00:34:08,350 --> 00:34:12,572
[coughing]
I like a little heat,
but this is overpowering.

808
00:34:12,659 --> 00:34:14,661
Whoa.

809
00:34:14,748 --> 00:34:16,141
We didn't add any heat.

810
00:34:16,228 --> 00:34:17,838
Whew! That would be me.

811
00:34:18,926 --> 00:34:20,014
Sorry.

812
00:34:20,101 --> 00:34:22,147
Fire! Fire!

813
00:34:24,453 --> 00:34:26,194
The Red Savina pepper

814
00:34:26,281 --> 00:34:29,415
ah, I got it
from Julia's pantry.

815
00:34:29,502 --> 00:34:31,460
I thought
it would be appropriate

816
00:34:31,547 --> 00:34:34,072
'and I'm just mad
for heat, so..'

817
00:34:34,159 --> 00:34:35,682
'Obviously, I was
a little heavy-handed.'

818
00:34:35,769 --> 00:34:38,641
My-my apologies.

819
00:34:38,728 --> 00:34:39,990
What's the trouble, Eliza?

820
00:34:40,078 --> 00:34:42,036
I don't, um..

821
00:34:42,123 --> 00:34:43,907
I don't feel well.
I should go.

822
00:34:43,994 --> 00:34:45,518
What's the problem?

823
00:34:45,605 --> 00:34:48,782
[Eliza]
'Uh, the peppers,
it didn't go down well.'

824
00:34:48,869 --> 00:34:50,479
The pepper?
What's wrong with the pepper?

825
00:34:50,566 --> 00:34:52,394
If you don't wanna talk
about it here

826
00:34:52,481 --> 00:34:53,526
we can take it down
to headquarters.

827
00:34:53,613 --> 00:34:54,831
How long can we keep her there?

828
00:34:54,918 --> 00:34:55,919
48 hours.

829
00:34:56,006 --> 00:34:57,573
Wow, that's a stretch of time.

830
00:34:57,660 --> 00:35:01,011
I have to leave.
I have to leave right now.

831
00:35:01,099 --> 00:35:02,709
[Patrick]
'No, that would
be rude. I don't think so.'

832
00:35:02,796 --> 00:35:04,493
What is wrong with you?

833
00:35:04,580 --> 00:35:08,628
We all have to go
to the hospital right now.

834
00:35:08,715 --> 00:35:10,151
[Teresa]
'What for?'

835
00:35:10,238 --> 00:35:14,155
The pepper...I think
it was poisoned.

836
00:35:14,242 --> 00:35:17,332
I-I-I saw Ludo put
something into it.

837
00:35:17,419 --> 00:35:19,073
I did not. How dare you!

838
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:21,467
Maybe you're just cramping
a little? Just a little--

839
00:35:21,554 --> 00:35:24,948
Alright, alright.
I poisoned Julia's pepper.

840
00:35:25,035 --> 00:35:26,211
And Jeffrey's gin.

841
00:35:26,298 --> 00:35:28,648
Yes, yes! Just bring me
to a hospital.

842
00:35:28,735 --> 00:35:30,606
No, hospitals
are terrible germ factories.

843
00:35:30,693 --> 00:35:32,956
- 'You don't wanna go there.'
- Are you insane?

844
00:35:33,043 --> 00:35:35,698
Relax. We knew about the ricin
in the pepper.

845
00:35:35,785 --> 00:35:37,439
We just didn't know
who put it there.

846
00:35:37,526 --> 00:35:39,224
I don't understand.
Are we poisoned?

847
00:35:39,311 --> 00:35:41,443
No. I switched the peppers.

848
00:35:41,530 --> 00:35:42,618
[all sighs]

849
00:35:42,705 --> 00:35:43,967
Clever of me.

850
00:35:44,054 --> 00:35:45,186
[whispering]
God.

851
00:35:47,188 --> 00:35:49,582
[sighs]

852
00:35:49,669 --> 00:35:52,672
Okay, so we know that your
restaurant didn't fail, Eliza.

853
00:35:52,759 --> 00:35:54,848
Jeffrey and Julia,
they killed it.

854
00:35:54,935 --> 00:35:57,416
We did some digging
on Julia's father.

855
00:35:57,503 --> 00:35:59,592
He's the one who squashed
your liquor license.

856
00:35:59,679 --> 00:36:01,376
Jeffrey was a drunk.

857
00:36:01,463 --> 00:36:04,074
He needed you in his kitchen,
so he asked his lover

858
00:36:04,162 --> 00:36:07,774
if she could have her big,
powerful daddy do her a favor.

859
00:36:07,861 --> 00:36:11,125
He didn't call you that night to talk about recipes, did he?

860
00:36:11,212 --> 00:36:15,825
He called to apologize for sabotaging your liquor license,

861
00:36:15,912 --> 00:36:18,915
for ruining any chance you had at opening your own place.

862
00:36:20,482 --> 00:36:21,962
But I..

863
00:36:23,790 --> 00:36:26,488
I am trying to become
a better man.

864
00:36:28,273 --> 00:36:29,970
I am.

865
00:36:30,057 --> 00:36:31,711
He told you what he did

866
00:36:31,798 --> 00:36:35,584
and then you,
decided to take your revenge.

867
00:36:37,934 --> 00:36:40,894
So you got some castor beans,
mixed up a batch of ricin

868
00:36:40,981 --> 00:36:42,983
and went to the resort
to find him.

869
00:36:47,683 --> 00:36:48,728
Yeah.

870
00:36:48,815 --> 00:36:50,469
[scoffs]

871
00:36:50,556 --> 00:36:52,253
I went to his room.

872
00:36:53,689 --> 00:36:54,821
[knock on door]

873
00:37:00,522 --> 00:37:02,002
Oh, Jeff.

874
00:37:03,525 --> 00:37:05,788
I accept your apology.

875
00:37:08,661 --> 00:37:10,750
Let me just, wash up.

876
00:37:12,230 --> 00:37:14,841
[Eliza]
He was such a fool.

877
00:37:14,928 --> 00:37:18,192
Like I was gonna let that go?

878
00:37:18,279 --> 00:37:20,803
Like we were just gonna make up and all be friends again?

879
00:37:20,890 --> 00:37:23,893
That's just...ridiculous.

880
00:37:27,332 --> 00:37:29,290
[Teresa]
And then you
went to the pantry

881
00:37:29,377 --> 00:37:30,726
and, uh, you found
Julia's pepper

882
00:37:30,813 --> 00:37:32,989
and you dosed that, too.

883
00:37:35,862 --> 00:37:39,996
One time I was cleaning shrimp
for Jeffrey.

884
00:37:40,083 --> 00:37:41,737
There were, like,
40 pounds of it

885
00:37:41,824 --> 00:37:44,392
and it was still half frozen,
but that was the job.

886
00:37:46,699 --> 00:37:50,877
'I couldn't feel my hands.
It was so cold.'

887
00:37:50,964 --> 00:37:55,011
I realized I was cutting
the flesh off my own fingers.

888
00:37:57,884 --> 00:37:59,755
Carving it off.

889
00:38:03,585 --> 00:38:05,195
And I didn't mind

890
00:38:06,327 --> 00:38:08,024
because Jeffrey
had a vision.

891
00:38:16,511 --> 00:38:18,208
I had a vision.

892
00:38:21,299 --> 00:38:25,738
And he said, go, babe.
You deserve your own place.

893
00:38:29,263 --> 00:38:31,352
But he was lying.

894
00:38:34,355 --> 00:38:36,314
Him and his..

895
00:38:38,490 --> 00:38:41,144
...rich bitch girlfriend.

896
00:38:41,231 --> 00:38:43,451
Like I was gonna
let that go.

897
00:38:51,503 --> 00:38:53,331
He said, I'm sorry.

898
00:38:56,334 --> 00:38:57,813
I'm sorry.

899
00:39:02,949 --> 00:39:04,124
I'm sorry?

900
00:39:05,995 --> 00:39:08,128
What good is that?

901
00:39:16,441 --> 00:39:18,660
[Abigail]
'Agent Lisbon, a word.'

902
00:39:20,314 --> 00:39:23,099
- How can I help you?
- You framed me.

903
00:39:23,186 --> 00:39:25,275
You put those castor beans
in my house, didn't you?

904
00:39:25,363 --> 00:39:26,886
Ma'am, on my word of honor

905
00:39:26,973 --> 00:39:30,193
I did not know that
those beans were planted.

906
00:39:30,280 --> 00:39:32,544
Mr. Jane was responsible.
I should've known.

907
00:39:32,631 --> 00:39:34,110
I should've stopped him,
and I didn't

908
00:39:34,197 --> 00:39:36,939
and for that,
I am really sorry.

909
00:39:37,026 --> 00:39:38,680
Why?

910
00:39:38,767 --> 00:39:40,552
He thought in order to catch
the killer

911
00:39:40,639 --> 00:39:42,902
that somebody else had to be
suspected of the crime.

912
00:39:42,989 --> 00:39:46,384
He's probably right.
I know you must be angry.

913
00:39:46,471 --> 00:39:47,907
[sighs]

914
00:39:47,994 --> 00:39:50,997
Trying to leave
the anger behind.

915
00:39:51,084 --> 00:39:53,956
Though that's pretty much
impossible, isn't it?

916
00:39:54,043 --> 00:39:55,218
It's hard.

917
00:39:55,305 --> 00:39:56,916
[sighs]

918
00:39:57,003 --> 00:39:59,832
Anyway, thank you
for being honest with me.

919
00:40:01,747 --> 00:40:05,707
Tell Mr. Jane he's
a mean, irresponsible sadist.

920
00:40:05,794 --> 00:40:07,448
'Oh, I will.'

921
00:40:10,799 --> 00:40:12,410
Ms. Barge?

922
00:40:16,109 --> 00:40:18,981
Living with an alcoholic
is hard.

923
00:40:19,068 --> 00:40:22,332
Some never change.
They don't even want to.

924
00:40:22,420 --> 00:40:24,857
But your husband checked
into treatment two weeks ago

925
00:40:24,944 --> 00:40:28,469
right after you told him
about the baby.

926
00:40:28,556 --> 00:40:31,211
I think he wanted to be there
for you and your child.

927
00:40:33,039 --> 00:40:36,738
That's why he ended
the relationship with Julia.

928
00:40:36,825 --> 00:40:39,437
For what it's worth, it seemed
like he wanted to change.

929
00:40:43,179 --> 00:40:44,790
Thank you.

930
00:40:46,748 --> 00:40:49,621
[dramatic music]

931
00:40:53,320 --> 00:40:55,061
It's okay. She's gone.

932
00:40:56,236 --> 00:40:58,020
- Who's gone?
- 'Oh, please.'

933
00:40:58,107 --> 00:41:00,153
Your guilty conscience
is obvious and well deserved.

934
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:03,548
Meh. Justice can be
a harsh mistress.

935
00:41:03,635 --> 00:41:05,593
Omelets and eggs,
ends to means

936
00:41:05,680 --> 00:41:07,247
all's well that ends well.

937
00:41:07,334 --> 00:41:09,379
A glib answer's no excuse.

938
00:41:11,904 --> 00:41:13,688
Oh, and Abigail said
to tell you

939
00:41:13,775 --> 00:41:15,995
that you are
a mean, irresponsible sadist.

940
00:41:17,953 --> 00:41:20,260
Well, that's pretty accurate.

941
00:41:21,696 --> 00:41:22,828
'You okay?'

942
00:41:24,177 --> 00:41:25,787
No point denying it to me.

943
00:41:25,874 --> 00:41:27,354
I'm fine.

944
00:41:27,441 --> 00:41:28,660
Okay.

945
00:41:29,661 --> 00:41:31,227
Goodnight.

946
00:41:34,230 --> 00:41:37,103
[instrumental music]

947
00:42:15,184 --> 00:42:17,622
[Patrick]
Stay tuned for scenes
from our next episode.

948
00:42:20,494 --> 00:42:23,062
[theme music]

